{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>But if there is harm.</b> For the woman. And there is a dispute: they say that the striker dies, because 'one who intends to kill his fellow is executed,' and their proof is that for the children it is written 'punished he shall be punished,' while for the death of the woman it is written 'and you shall give a life for a life.' Now if this is a punishment, what advantage is there in the death of the woman over the children? And if we say they are all cases of punishment, why did the verse change to say 'a life for a life'? And another dispute says that one who intends to kill this one and kills that one is not executed, and the verse changed to say 'a life for a life' because the punishment for a life is far greater than for the children. And their proof is the verse that comes after it: 'an eye for an eye,' which is not according to its plain sense but is rather a ransom, and this passage is mentioned on account of 'an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth,' for after it is written the judgment of the slave's eye and tooth."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:03.364737453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}